---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-fatigue`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Fatigue',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Fatigue', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Fatigue: When Correcting Deficiency Actually Fixes Tiredness"
  description="What the evidence shows about vitamin D and fatigue — the ViDiFatiCS trial, chronic fatigue, and how to know if your fatigue is likely to respond to correction."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-fatigue", label: "Vitamin D and Fatigue" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Fatigue</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Fatigue is one of the most common symptoms associated with vitamin D deficiency
      in observational studies, and one of the outcomes patients most hope
      supplementation will fix. The reality is nuanced — correction helps some
      people, particularly the severely deficient, and doesn't help many others.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The ViDiFatiCS trial</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Nowak 2016 randomised 120 adults with self-reported fatigue and 25(OH)D
      &lt; 20 ng/mL to either 100,000 IU cholecalciferol bolus or placebo. At
      4 weeks, the vitamin D group had significantly greater fatigue reduction
      on validated fatigue scales. This trial specifically enrolled the deficient
      — the population most likely to respond.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Meta-analyses</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      2020 Cochrane-style pooled analysis of RCTs of vitamin D for fatigue: modest
      benefit overall, with effect size larger in patients starting below 20 ng/mL
      and negligible in those starting above 30 ng/mL. Consistent with the general
      pattern of vitamin D trials.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Chronic fatigue syndrome / ME/CFS</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D deficiency is common in ME/CFS cohorts, but supplementation trials
      have not shown reliable disease-modifying benefit. Correcting deficiency is
      reasonable general care; ME/CFS specific management is a specialist area with
      distinct interventions (pacing, symptom-directed therapy).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fatigue causes not fixed by vitamin D</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Poor sleep hygiene or untreated sleep apnoea.</li>
      <li>Iron-deficiency anaemia (especially in menstruating women; see our <a href="/iron" class="text-amber-700 underline">iron page</a>).</li>
      <li>Untreated hypothyroidism (check TSH).</li>
      <li>Depression or anxiety.</li>
      <li>Overtraining or under-recovery in athletes.</li>
      <li>Occult chronic disease (diabetes, kidney disease, cancer).</li>
      <li>Medications with fatigue as side effect (beta-blockers, antihistamines, statins).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical approach</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Persistent fatigue &gt; 1 month: check 25(OH)D, TSH, CBC (for anaemia), ferritin, B12, glucose.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D &lt; 20 ng/mL: correct with 2,000–4,000 IU/day for 8–12 weeks; expect partial improvement in a subset of patients.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D &gt; 30 ng/mL and still fatigued: look elsewhere; more vitamin D won't help.</li>
      <li>Persistent unexplained fatigue: comprehensive medical workup, not another supplement.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Fatigue with weight loss, night sweats,
      new severe headaches, chest pain, or bleeding needs urgent medical evaluation,
      not a supplement trial.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-fatigue" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
